<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Mark</h1><p class="st-meta">1198 CE – 1807 CE (approx.) · capital Hamm</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Westphalian county around Hamm and the later Ruhr, whose counts beat Cologne at Worringen and whose iron trades made it an industrial heartland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A branch of the counts of Altena acquired the castle of Mark near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamm">Hamm</a> and from about 1198 called themselves counts of the Mark. Adolf I founded Hamm in 1226. Their county lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippe-river">Lippe</a>, and they long fought the archbishops of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> for dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-ii-count-of-the-mark">Eberhard II</a> helped defeat <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a> in 1288, securing the county's independence. Small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamm">Hamm</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unna">Unna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iserlohn">Iserlohn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludenscheid">Lüdenscheid</a> grew from metalworking and wire-drawing. In 1368 the counts also inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, and from 1398 Cleves and Mark were ruled together. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soest">Soest</a> joined Mark after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soest-feud">Soest Feud</a> of 1444 to 1449.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Joined with Jülich-Berg in 1521, Mark was inherited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1614 after the Jülich succession dispute. It was part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a> from 1807, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> towns industrialised in the 19th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-mark.jpg" alt="County of Mark" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: ZH2000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_County_of_Mark_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1198 CE – 1807 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hamm</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adolf I of the Mark, Eberhard II of the Mark, Engelbert III of the Mark</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Westphalian Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mark, Grafschaft Mark</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=county-of-mark" aria-label="County of Mark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4857 390 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Mark lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5177 646L5229 694M5177 646L5139 643M5177 646L5282 625M5177 646L5154 659"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5139 643h0M5282 625h0M5154 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5177 646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5177 646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hamm. <a href="/atlas?year=1502&amp;focus=county-of-mark">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
